{
  "evidence": "A: Massive Care investments—NHS revenue up to £28bn by 2030; guaranteed NHS dentistry; 28‑day access to mental health therapies; school counsellor in every school; free personal care with £20bn/year; disability benefits +5%; end sanctions, +£40/week to UC, scrap two‑child cap; 150,000 social homes/year, end no‑fault evictions and enable rent controls; free school meals for all; decriminalise sex work; repeal protest‑curbing laws; support assisted dying. Equality‑oriented measures—scrap tuition fees and restore grants; long‑term move to UBI; wealth and windfall taxes; PR and rights protections; end hostile environment and create safe routes.\nB: Authority/loyalty‑oriented measures—Mandatory National Service; tougher protest controls; 8,000 more neighbourhood police with hotspot policing; tougher sentences and four new prisons; legal annual cap on migration with rising thresholds, dependants restrictions, monthly removals to Rwanda; stronger welfare sanctions, tighten work capability and PIP reforms. Care measures are more limited—NHS rises above inflation, recruitment targets, tech upgrades and dental plan; childcare expansion; but no major welfare uprates or housing protections.",
  "reasoning": "Given the electorate’s high Care priority and some Equality concern, A aligns better by prioritising large-scale NHS, mental health, social care, disability, anti-poverty and housing protections, alongside egalitarian reforms, while showing low Sanctity emphasis (e.g., assisted dying, decriminalisation). B emphasises Authority and order (national service, tougher policing/protest controls, migration caps, harsher sentencing) and tighter welfare conditionality, which fits a higher‑authority profile rather than the electorate’s care‑led, low‑sanctity orientation.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "A"
}
